</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 8, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A number field <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive if <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are the only subextensions of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call an algebraic point <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> primitive if the number
field <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive. We present several sets of sufficient conditions
for a curve <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to have finitely many
primitive points of a given degree <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, let <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperelliptic curve of genus <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that the Jacobian <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is simple. We show that <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has only finitely many primitive degree <math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points,
and in particular it has only finitely many degree <math id="abstract1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points with Galois group <math id="abstract1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="abstract1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, for any even <math id="abstract1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>,
a hyperelliptic curve <math id="abstract1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> has infinitely many imprimitive
degree <math id="abstract1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points whose Galois group is a subgroup of
<math id="abstract1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}\wr S_{d/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}\wr S_{d/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Curves, Jacobians, primitive points
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>11G30
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By a <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">curve</span> <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> over a field <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> we mean a smooth projective
and geometrically irreducible variety defined over <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> having
dimension <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that a curve <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> defined over a field <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>
is <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hyperelliptic</span> if the genus of <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
is at least <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> admits a degree <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
morphism <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined
over <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, which we shall refer to as the
<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hyperelliptic morphism</span>.
We say that <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/K</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-gonality</span> <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the least degree of a non-constant morphism
<math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, for example, a hyperelliptic curve defined over <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> has
<math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/K</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bielliptic</span> if its genus is
at least <math id="S1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and it admits a degree <math id="S1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> morphism
<math id="S1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow E</annotation></semantics></math>, defined over <math id="S1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of genus <math id="S1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a number field
<math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">imprimitive</span>
if there is some subextension <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}\subsetneq L\subsetneq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⊊</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>⊊</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}\subsetneq L\subsetneq K</annotation></semantics></math>;
if there is no such subextension then <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is called
<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">primitive</span>. Now let <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C</annotation></semantics></math> be an algebraic point; i.e. <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">degree <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span>
if the number field <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">primitive</span>
if the number field <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive, otherwise
we say that <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">imprimitive</span>.
A degree <math id="S1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point <math id="S1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is called <math id="S1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-isolated</span> if there is no degree <math id="S1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
non-constant morphism <math id="S1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
defined over <math id="S1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is in the preimage
of an element of <math id="S1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The notion of isolated points was introduced
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and has become
important in understanding low degree points on curves,
particularly on modular curves (e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>).
It is easy to see that if <math id="S1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality of <math id="S1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated. A key observation we make in
this paper is that primitive points
are often <math id="S1.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated even if the degree is greater than the gonality.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
with genus <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\neq m,\qquad d\,&lt;\,1+\frac{g}{m-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.448em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\neq m,\qquad d\,&lt;\,1+\frac{g}{m-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
that is not <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated. Then <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a subfield of index <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1&lt;d^{\prime}&lt;d,\qquad d^{\prime}\mid\gcd(d,m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>gcd</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;d^{\prime}&lt;d,\qquad d^{\prime}\mid\gcd(d,m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is prime then any degree </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> point
</span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isolated.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a primitive
degree </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> point, then </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isolated.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under certain additional assumptions on the Jacobian <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> of the curve <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is possible to conclude finiteness of primitive degree <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
with genus <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Let <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose either of the following hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">or </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
and </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite for every abelian subvariety </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of dimension </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely many primitive degree <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points. Moreover, if <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime then
<math id="Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely many degree <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that, for <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math>, assumption (b) is trivially satisfied
if <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is simple.
We moreover note that the inequality <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math> in (b) follows immediately
from (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) if <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3</span></span><span id="Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the modular curve <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(239)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(239)</annotation></semantics></math> which has genus <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=20</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=6</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Table 3]</cite>).
We consider degree <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points for <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>;
we note that (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is satisfied
for these values of <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
A straightforward computation in <span id="Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Magma</span>,
which makes use of modular symbols algorithms due to Cremona <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and Stein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>,
shows that the Jacobian
<math id="Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(239)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(239)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(239)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(239)</annotation></semantics></math> factors as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(239)\sim\mathcal{A}_{3}\times\mathcal{A}_{17}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>17</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(239)\sim\mathcal{A}_{3}\times\mathcal{A}_{17},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{17}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>17</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{17}</annotation></semantics></math> are
simple abelian varieties of dimension <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Moreover <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{17}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>17</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{17}</annotation></semantics></math> has analytic rank <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> has positive analytic rank. Assuming the Birch and Swinnerton–Dyer
conjecture,
<math id="Thmthm3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(239)(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(239)(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite, and so hypothesis (a) of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
is not satisfied.
However, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(239)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(239)</annotation></semantics></math>,
clearly satisfies hypothesis (b) for <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,
we conclude that <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(239)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>239</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(239)</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely many quadratic, cubic
and primitive quartic points.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We point out the following theorem
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Proposition 2.3]</cite>
which gives a stronger conclusion than Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
under different assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Derickx and Sutherland)<span id="Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
with genus <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
is finite. Suppose <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely
many degree <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The following corollary to Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is obtained by restricting
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and its proof to the hyperelliptic case.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5</span></span><span id="Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperelliptic curve defined over <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
with genus <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian
of <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer.
Suppose either of the following hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">or </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
and </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite for every abelian subvariety </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of dimension </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
has finitely many primitive degree <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.
More precisely, the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is odd, then </span><math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
has finitely many degree </span><math id="S1.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> points.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is even, then
for all but finitely many
degree </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> points </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
the field </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains a subfield of index </span><math id="S1.I4.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I4.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We note that Gunther and Morrow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Proposition 2.6]</cite>
prove that conclusions (i) and (ii) hold for <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> hyperelliptic curves over <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
with a rational Weierstrass point, provided only that <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math>, though their proof
is rather different.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we note the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6</span></span><span id="Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperelliptic curve defined over <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> be an even integer. Then <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
has infinitely many imprimitive degree <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may suppose <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has an affine model</span></p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\;:\;Y^{2}=F(X)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\;:\;Y^{2}=F(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathbb{Q}[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathbb{Q}[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is a squarefree polynomial.
Let <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be any number field of degree <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/2</annotation></semantics></math>
and choose <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in L</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Faltings’ theorem, <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. Thus
we may choose some <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta+a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta+a)</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-square in <math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(\theta+a,\sqrt{F(\theta+a)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\theta+a,\sqrt{F(\theta+a)})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a degree <math id="S1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="S1.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
and is imprimitive as <math id="S1.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> contains the index <math id="S1.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
subfield <math id="S1.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C</annotation></semantics></math> be an algebraic point; i.e. <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the <span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Galois group of <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math></span>
to be the Galois group of the Galois closure
of <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. A degree <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> point whose
Galois group is <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive (Lemma <a href="#Thmthm29" title="Lemma 29. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>).
Thus if <math id="S1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is an even integer and if <math id="S1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the
hypotheses of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> then <math id="S1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has only finitely
many degree <math id="S1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points with Galois group <math id="S1.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, it follows from the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
that <math id="S1.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has infinitely many degree <math id="S1.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points
with Galois group contained in <math id="S1.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}\wr S_{d/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}\wr S_{d/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In a separate paper
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> we explore Galois groups of
algebraic points in more detail. For now, we content
ourselves with the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperelliptic curve defined over <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with genus <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is
trivial. Then <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has no quartic points with Galois group <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has infinitely many quartic points with
Galois group contained in <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The above results exploit the gonality map <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to
make deductions about low degree primitive points.
However, the existence of a low degree map <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of positive genus
also makes it more likely for low degree primitive algebraic points on <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to
be <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated, as illustrated by the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of curves defined over <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for the genera of <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively, and suppose <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\,&lt;\,1+\frac{g-mg^{\prime}}{m-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.448em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\,&lt;\,1+\frac{g-mg^{\prime}}{m-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
that is not <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated. Then <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>
contains a subfield of index <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1&lt;d^{\prime}&lt;d,\qquad d^{\prime}\mid\gcd(d,m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>gcd</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;d^{\prime}&lt;d,\qquad d^{\prime}\mid\gcd(d,m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm8.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm8.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is prime then any degree </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> point
</span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I5.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isolated.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a primitive
degree </span><math id="S1.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> point, then </span><math id="S1.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S1.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I5.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isolated.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of curves defined over <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for the genera of <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively, and suppose <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Write <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> for the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.
Then <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely many primitive degree <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points. Moreover, if <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime then
<math id="Thmthm9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely many degree <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 10</span></span><span id="Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> by giving an example.
Consider the modular curve <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LMFDB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> gives the following information:</p>
<ol id="S1.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math> has genus <math id="S1.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math> is a degree <math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> cover of a genus <math id="S1.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> curve (the latter has
<span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LMFDB</span> label <span id="S1.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">45.576.9-45.a.4.1</span>);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math> has analytic rank <math id="S1.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Write <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=J_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=J_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> has analytic rank <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, a theorem of Kato <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Corollary 14.3]</cite>
implies that the Mordell–Weil group <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. Thus, by
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, the curve
<math id="Thmthm10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math> has only finitely many degree <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
points for <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>,
and only finitely many primitive degree <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points for <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=6</annotation></semantics></math>.
We point out that the <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality of <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(45)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>45</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(45)</annotation></semantics></math> appears to be currently
unknown; according to the <span id="Thmthm10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LMFDB</span> it belongs to the interval <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\leq\gamma\leq 18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\leq\gamma\leq 18</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Applying Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and its proof to bielliptic curves
gives the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 11</span></span><span id="Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a bielliptic curve defined over <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with genus <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.
Let <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq d\leq g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq d\leq g-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely many primitive degree <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.
More precisely, the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is odd, then </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has finitely many degree </span><math id="S1.I7.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> points.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and even, then for
all but finitely many
degree </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> points </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
the field </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains
a subfield of index </span><math id="S1.I7.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I7.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The above theorems are concerned with finiteness criteria
for low degree primitive points.
In the opposite direction, we show that if the degree <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large compared to the genus,
then there are infinitely many primitive degree <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points, provided there is at least one such point.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12</span></span><span id="Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve.
Let <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq g+1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose there exists a primitive
degree <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there are infinitely many primitive
degree <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
we review some standard results regarding Riemann–Roch spaces
and we use these to prove Theorem <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Proofs of Theorems 
and ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we recall the Castelnuovo–Severi
theorem and use it
to give proofs of Theorems <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
and <a href="#Thmthm8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>
we give criteria for when
a complete linear series does not contain
any primitive divisors (a primitive divisor
is simply the Galois orbit of a primitive algebraic
point).
Let <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the hypotheses of either Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
or Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>,
and write <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-th
symmetric power of <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>,
using a powerful theorem of Faltings,
we show that <math id="S1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> may be decomposed as a finite
union of complete linear series.
In Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Proofs of Theorems  and 
and their corollaries ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
we prove Theorems <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
and their corollaries, making use of the results
developed in previous sections.
In Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> we recall the relationship
between primitive extensions and primitive group actions,
and we use this together with Hilbert’s irreducibility
theorem to give a proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.
Finally, in Sections <a href="#S8" title="8. Low degree primitive points on some 
        
          
            ⁢
            
              
              X
              1
            
            
              
              
                (
                N
                ) ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S9" title="9. Low degree primitive points on some 
        
          
            ⁢
            
              
              X
              0
            
            
              
              
                (
                N
                ) ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
we give consequences of Theorems <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
and <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> (and their proofs) for the modular curves
<math id="S1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for certain small <math id="S1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to Nils Bruin, Victor Flynn, Samuel Le Fourn, David Loeffler, Filip Najman,
Petar Orlić,
Damiano Testa and Bianca Viray for helpful discussions,
and to the referees for suggesting many improvements.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined
over <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. When we speak of divisors on <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> we in fact mean rational divisors:
a <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">divisor</span> on <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite formal integral linear combination <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\sum a_{i}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\sum a_{i}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of algebraic points <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that is stable
under the action of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call this divisor <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">effective</span> and write
<math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. An <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">irreducible divisor</span> is an effective divisor that
cannot be written as the sum of two non-zero effective divisors. Thus an effective degree <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisor <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
is irreducible if and only if there is a degree <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=P_{1}+P_{2}+\cdots+P_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=P_{1}+P_{2}+\cdots+P_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{1},\dotsc,P_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{1},\dotsc,P_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Galois orbit of <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the
irreducible divisor corresponding</span> to <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a divisor <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> we denote by
<math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(D)</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Riemann–Roch space</span> defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(D)\;=\;\{0\}\cup\{f\in\mathbb{Q}(C)^{\times}\;:\;\divv(f)+D\geq 0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(D)\;=\;\{0\}\cup\{f\in\mathbb{Q}(C)^{\times}\;:\;\divv(f)+D\geq 0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and we let <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)=\dim L(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)=\dim L(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We shall make frequent use of the Riemann–Roch theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, page 13]</cite> which
asserts that</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)-\ell(K_{C}-D)\;=\;\deg(D)-g+1;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)-\ell(K_{C}-D)\;=\;\deg(D)-g+1;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">here <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is any canonical divisor on <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(K_{C})=2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(K_{C})=2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, if <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(D)\geq 2g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(D)\geq 2g-1</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{C}-D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{C}-D</annotation></semantics></math> has negative degree and cannot be linearly equivalent to an
effective divisor. In that case <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(K_{C}-D)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(K_{C}-D)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We shall also require
Clifford’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Theorem IV.5.4]</cite> on special
effective divisors.
Recall that
an effective divisor <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">special</span> if <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(K_{C}-D)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(K_{C}-D)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Clifford)<span id="Thmthm13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective special divisor on a curve <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)\;\leq\;\frac{\deg(D)}{2}\,+\,1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.392em">+</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)\;\leq\;\frac{\deg(D)}{2}\,+\,1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, equality occurs if and only if <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=0</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a canonical divisor,
or <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is hyperelliptic and <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of a hyperelliptic divisor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a hyperelliptic curve <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped
with a degree <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> morphism <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>;
a <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hyperelliptic divisor</span> on <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Theorem 7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
We may suppose <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has an affine model as in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
where <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathbb{Q}[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathbb{Q}[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is a squarefree polynomial of degree <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+2</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmthm6" title="Lemma 6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
and its proof that <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has infinitely many quartic points with
Galois group contained in <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To complete the proof it is enough to show that there
are no quartic points on <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with Galois group <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(F)=2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(F)=2g+1</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> be the single point
at infinity on this model, and write <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}=4\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}=4\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(F)=2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(F)=2g+2</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> be the two points at infinity,
and write <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}=2\infty_{+}+2\infty_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}=2\infty_{+}+2\infty_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In either case <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is twice a hyperelliptic divisor.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding irreducible divisor.
Since <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D-D_{0}\,\sim\,0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D-D_{0}\,\sim\,0</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> denotes linear equivalence on <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
That is,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=D_{0}+\divv(f)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=D_{0}+\divv(f),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,X,X^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,X,X^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-basis of <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us first assume our claim and use it to complete the proof.
Thus <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=a_{0}+a_{1}X+a_{2}X^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=a_{0}+a_{1}X+a_{2}X^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover,
<math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is non-constant as <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\neq D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\neq D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <math id="S2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a zero of <math id="S2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(P)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
the non-constant polynomial <math id="S2.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}+a_{1}U+a_{2}U^{2}\in\mathbb{Q}[U]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}+a_{1}U+a_{2}U^{2}\in\mathbb{Q}[U]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)=\mathbb{Q}(X(P),Y(P))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)=\mathbb{Q}(X(P),Y(P))</annotation></semantics></math>
is a quartic field, and <math id="S2.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y(P)^{2}=F(X(P))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(P)^{2}=F(X(P))</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that <math id="S2.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(X(P))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(X(P))</annotation></semantics></math>
is quadratic and contained in the quartic field <math id="S2.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the
Galois group of <math id="S2.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is neither <math id="S2.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S2.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove our claim. Note that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a double pole
at infinity and no other poles if <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(F)=2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(F)=2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>;
and also
<math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a simple pole at <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, and has no other poles
if <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(F)=2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(F)=2g+2</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,
<math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is enough to show that <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now make use of our assumption that <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> then
Riemann–Roch immediately gives <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then Riemann–Roch tells us that <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is special,
and since it is twice a hyperelliptic divisor, Clifford’s theorem
gives the equality <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Proofs of Theorems <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
and <a href="#Thmthm8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by recalling the classical Castelnuovo–Severi theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 14</span></span><span id="Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Castelnuovo–Severi theorem)<span id="Thmthm14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a perfect field, and let <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>
be curves over <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the genera of these
curves by <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively. Let <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Y}:X\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Y}:X\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}:X\rightarrow Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}:X\rightarrow Z</annotation></semantics></math>
be non-constant morphisms defined over <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,
having degrees <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
Suppose</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)\;&gt;\;m\cdot g(Y)+n\cdot g(Z)+(m-1)(n-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)\;&gt;\;m\cdot g(Y)+n\cdot g(Z)+(m-1)(n-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm14.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm14.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there is a curve <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and a morphism
<math id="Thmthm14.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
also defined over <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
and of degree <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> through which both <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
factor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We are unable to find a reference that gives the precise statement
that we need. Indeed the theorem is most
often given in the context of complex Riemann surfaces (for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>),
or the field of definition of the morphism <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\rightarrow X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\rightarrow X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
is not mentioned (for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, Corollary]</cite>). We therefore give an explanation of how
the version above follows straightforwardly
from a proof due to Mattuck and Tate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>
of the Castelnuovo–Severi inequality.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=Y\times Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=Y\times Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Given two divisors <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the surface <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>,
we denote their intersection number by <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\cdot D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\cdot D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>
under the morphism <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{Y},\pi_{Z}):X\rightarrow S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{Y},\pi_{Z}):X\rightarrow S</annotation></semantics></math>,
and write <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:X\rightarrow D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:X\rightarrow D</annotation></semantics></math> for the induced map.
Clearly the curve <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and the map <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>
are defined over <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:D\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:D\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:D\rightarrow Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:D\rightarrow Z</annotation></semantics></math> be the restrictions
of the projections <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\times Z\rightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\times Z\rightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\times Z\rightarrow Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\times Z\rightarrow Z</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="S3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Y}=u\circ h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Y}=u\circ h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Z}=v\circ h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Z}=v\circ h</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that <math id="S3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(h)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(h)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. The theorem follows from our claim
on letting <math id="S3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normalization of <math id="S3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove our
claim by contradiction. Suppose <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(h)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(h)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is a birational map,
and <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(D)=g(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(D)=g(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(D)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the geometric genus of <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>).
Mattuck and Tate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, page 296]</cite>
show that</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\cdot D\;\leq\;2mn,\qquad D\cdot K_{S}\;=\;(2g(Y)-2)m+(2g(Z)-2)n," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\cdot D\;\leq\;2mn,\qquad D\cdot K_{S}\;=\;(2g(Y)-2)m+(2g(Z)-2)n,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes any canonical divisor on <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> (the inequality
on the left is itself referred to as the Castelnuovo–Severi inequality). By the adjunction formula for surfaces
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, Exercise V.1.3]</cite> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p_{a}(D)-2\;=\;D\cdot(D+K_{S})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p_{a}(D)-2\;=\;D\cdot(D+K_{S}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is the arithmetic genus of <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, from (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 3. Proofs of Theorems 
and ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(D)\;\leq\;g(Y)m+g(Z)n+(m-1)(n-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(D)\;\leq\;g(Y)m+g(Z)n+(m-1)(n-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">However, since the geometric genus is bounded by the arithmetic genus,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)\;=\;g(D)\;\leq\;p_{a}(D)\;\leq\;g(Y)m+g(Z)n+(m-1)(n-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)\;=\;g(D)\;\leq\;p_{a}(D)\;\leq\;g(Y)m+g(Z)n+(m-1)(n-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">contradicting assumption (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Theorem 14 (Castelnuovo–Severi theorem). ‣ 3. Proofs of Theorems 
and ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
This establishes our claim.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
is not <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated. Thus there is a degree <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
map <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined over <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(P)=\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(P)=\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe that <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> consists precisely
of the Galois orbit of <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality of <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>; thus
in particular, there is a morphism <math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
defined over <math id="S3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, of minimal degree <math id="S3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>),
the Castelnuovo–Severi theorem immediately implies that
the morphisms <math id="S3.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> must simultaneously factor through
a common non-trivial morphism of curves.
We obtain a commutative diagram of non-constant morphisms of curves</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.E7.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="67.76" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-34.82px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 186.03 67.76" width="186.03"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,67.76) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(93.02,0) translate(0,34.82)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -93.02 -27.88)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 65.88)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.52)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 41.49 0) translate(51.53,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.36 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 60.82)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(20.69,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.73 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="15.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 41.38 0) translate(51.64,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.47 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="11.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 111.45 0) translate(53.9,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.73 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="15.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -13.75 13.08 L -51.33 -10.97"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(-0.84227 -0.53906 0.53906 -0.84227 -51.57 -11.12)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.01 6.04)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 13.08 L 0 -12.53"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -12.8)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.26 -3.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces h}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 13.75 13.08 L 51.33 -10.97"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.84227 -0.53906 0.53906 0.84227 51.57 -11.12)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 36.03 4.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -18.71 -24.42 L -50.81 -24.42"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 -51.09 -24.42)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -38.31 -31.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces u}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 18.71 -24.42 L 50.81 -24.42"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 51.09 -24.42)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 32.09 -31.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E7.m1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces v}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">defined over <math id="S3.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(h)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(h)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="S3.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=\deg(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=\deg(h)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> divides
both <math id="S3.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\deg(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\deg(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\deg(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\deg(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}\mid\gcd(d,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}\mid\gcd(d,m)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=d</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\mid m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\mid m</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <math id="S3.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>
by the minimality of <math id="S3.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S3.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=d</annotation></semantics></math> contradicting
(<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We deduce that <math id="S3.p5.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p5.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=h(P)\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=h(P)\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p5.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
acts transitively on <math id="S3.p5.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(\alpha)\ni P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∋</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(\alpha)\ni P</annotation></semantics></math>, it acts transitively
on <math id="S3.p5.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}(\alpha)\ni Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∋</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}(\alpha)\ni Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.p5.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S3.p5.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(u)=d/d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(u)=d/d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.p5.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(Q)\subseteq\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(Q)\subseteq\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the field <math id="S3.p5.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S3.p5.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> contains the
subfield <math id="S3.p5.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> of index <math id="S3.p5.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The theorem follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">This proof is similar to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
As before, let <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a
degree <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated.
Thus there is a degree <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
map <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined over <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(P)=\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(P)=\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">From assumption (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>),
the Castelnuovo–Severi theorem gives
a commutative diagram of non-constant morphisms of curves</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.E8.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="67.76" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-34.82px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 185.17 67.76" width="185.17"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,67.76) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(92.58,0) translate(0,34.82)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -92.58 -27.88)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 65.88)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 14.52)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 41.49 0) translate(51.53,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.36 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 60.82)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(20.69,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.73 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.13em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="15.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbb{P}^{1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 41.38 0) translate(51.64,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.47 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="11.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces Y}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 111.45 0) translate(53.47,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.3 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="14.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces C^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -13.32 13.08 L -50.9 -10.97"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(-0.84227 -0.53906 0.53906 -0.84227 -51.13 -11.12)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.58 6.04)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces f}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0.43 13.08 L 0.43 -12.53"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0.43 -12.8)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.69 -3.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="6.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces h}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 14.1 13.08 L 52.79 -11.83"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.84079 -0.54137 0.54137 0.84079 53.03 -11.98)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 36.93 3.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -18.28 -24.42 L -50.38 -24.42"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 -50.65 -24.42)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -37.87 -31.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces u}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 19.14 -24.42 L 51.24 -24.42"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 51.52 -24.42)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 32.52 -31.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="5.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.E8.m1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces v}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">defined over <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(h)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(h)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
As before we let <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=\deg(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=\deg(h)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(u)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(u)=1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cong\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
which contradicts the existence of
a non-constant morphism <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
to the curve <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> having genus <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(u)&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(u)&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=d/\deg(u)&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=d/\deg(u)&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.
The rest of the proof is similar to the
proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve over <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an effective divisor <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
the notation <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">complete linear series
containing <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D\rvert=\{D+\divv(f):f\in L(D)\};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D\rvert=\{D+\divv(f):f\in L(D)\};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">this is precisely the set of effective divisors linearly equivalent to <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe that for effective divisors <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\sim D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\sim D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D\rvert=\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D\rvert=\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D\rvert\cong\mathbb{P}^{\ell(D)-1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D\rvert\cong\mathbb{P}^{\ell(D)-1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}\subseteq L(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}\subseteq L(D)</annotation></semantics></math> for any effective divisor <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D\rvert=\{D\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D\rvert=\{D\}</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if <math id="S4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following well-known lemma highlights the
relationship
between a point being not <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated
and the complete linear series of its irreducible
divisor having positive dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 15</span></span><span id="Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="Thmthm15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmthm15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible degree <math id="Thmthm15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisor.
The following are equivalent.</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\lvert D\rvert\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\lvert D\rvert\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a degree </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> morphism </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
defined over </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, such that </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(\infty)=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(\infty)=D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is not </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-isolated.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D\rvert\cong\mathbb{P}^{\ell(D)-1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D\rvert\cong\mathbb{P}^{\ell(D)-1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\lvert D\rvert=\ell(D)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\lvert D\rvert=\ell(D)-1</annotation></semantics></math>
and therefore (a) and (b) are equivalent.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is some non-constant <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L(D)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathbb{Q}(C)^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{Q}(C)^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv(f)+D\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv(f)+D\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>
for the divisor of poles of <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is non-constant,
<math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a rational divisor. Moreover, <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv_{\infty}(f)\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv_{\infty}(f)\leq D</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv(f)+D\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv(f)+D\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible, <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv_{\infty}(f)=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv_{\infty}(f)=D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now we consider <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as a morphism <math id="S4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(\infty)=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(\infty)=D</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S4.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> so does <math id="S4.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (b)
implies (c).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose (c). Then <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L(D)</annotation></semantics></math> and non-constant giving (b).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that (c) implies (d). To complete the proof
we suppose (d) and prove (c). Thus there is
a morphism <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
defined over <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(P)=\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(P)=\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Composing <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with an automorphism of <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we may
suppose <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\leq\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\leq\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is stable under the action
of Galois. Thus <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\divv_{\infty}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>div</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\divv_{\infty}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> as both divisors have degree <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We will call an irreducible divisor <span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">primitive</span>
if it is the Galois orbit of a primitive point.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 16</span></span><span id="Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve defined over <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with genus <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-gonality <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Let <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective degree <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisor
on <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D^{\prime})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D^{\prime})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> contains no
primitive degree <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors.
Moreover, if <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, then <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> contains no irreducible divisors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible divisor.
Then <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)=\ell(D^{\prime})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)=\ell(D^{\prime})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#Thmthm15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>,
any <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in D</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated.
By part (II) of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
we see that <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is imprimitive. Thus <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>
contains no primitive divisors.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose now that <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime.
Then part (I)
of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives a contradiction,
therefore <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> contains no irreducible
divisors.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The following variant of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm16" title="Corollary 16. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> has an almost
identical proof, but appealing to Theorem <a href="#Thmthm8" title="Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
instead of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 17</span></span><span id="Thmthm17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:C\rightarrow C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism of curves defined over <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for the genera of <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively, and suppose <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Let <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective degree <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisor
on <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D^{\prime})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D^{\prime})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> contains no
primitive degree <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors.
Moreover, if <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, then <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D^{\prime}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> contains no irreducible divisors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Decomposition of <math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> into complete linear series</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-th symmetric power of <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that
<math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with the set of
effective degree <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors on <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
The purpose of this section is to prove the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 18</span></span><span id="Thmthm18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve over <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer.
Suppose either of the following two conditions hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">or </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
and </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite for every abelian subvariety </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of dimension </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm18.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm18.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there are finitely many effective degree <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors
<math id="Thmthm18.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},D_{2},\dotsc,D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},D_{2},\dotsc,D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})\;=\;\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\lvert D_{i}\rvert." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})\;=\;\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\lvert D_{i}\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To prove the proposition we shall need the following famous theorem of Faltings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 19</span></span><span id="Thmthm19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Faltings)<span id="Thmthm19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian variety defined over a number field <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset B</annotation></semantics></math> be a subvariety defined over <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a finite number of abelian subvarieties
<math id="Thmthm19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\dotsc,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\dotsc,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, defined over <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and a finite number of points <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{m}\in V(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{m}\in V(K)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the translates <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}+B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}+B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are contained in <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,
and, moreover, such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(K)=\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}x_{i}+B_{i}(K)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(K)=\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}x_{i}+B_{i}(K).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We shall also need the following theorem of Debarre and Fahlaoui <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Corolllary 3.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 20</span></span><span id="Thmthm20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Debarre and Fahlaoui)<span id="Thmthm20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve of genus <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> with Jacobian <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed divisor of degree <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>
be the image of <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> under the Abel–Jacobi map</span></p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\;:\;C^{(d)}\rightarrow J,\qquad D\mapsto[D-D_{0}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\;:\;C^{(d)}\rightarrow J,\qquad D\mapsto[D-D_{0}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm20.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm20.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian subvariety of <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> with a translate contained in <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(A)\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(A)\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm18" title="Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>
then we take <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> and there is nothing to prove. So suppose
<math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and fix
<math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}\in C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}\in C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> be
the image of <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> under the Abel–Jacobi map (<a href="#S5.E11" title="In Theorem 20 (Debarre and Fahlaoui). ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>),
which is defined over <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.
This is trivially true if (a) holds, so suppose (b).
In particular <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math> and so <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>
is birational to <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Theorem 5.1]</cite>) and so has dimension <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
We apply Falting’s Theorem with <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=J</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=W_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=W_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, there are <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dotsc,x_{m}\in W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dotsc,x_{m}\in W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},\dotsc,B_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},\dotsc,B_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> abelian subvarieties of <math id="S5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}+B_{i}\subset W_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}+B_{i}\subset W_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})=\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}x_{i}+B_{i}(\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})=\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}x_{i}+B_{i}(\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">By the theorem of Debarre and Fahlaoui, <math id="S5.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(B_{i})\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(B_{i})\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, by assumption (b), <math id="S5.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. Hence
<math id="S5.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, proving our claim.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})=\{R_{1},\dotsc,R_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{d}(C)(\mathbb{Q})=\{R_{1},\dotsc,R_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and choose <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dotsc,D_{n}\in C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dotsc,D_{n}\in C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> mapping to <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dotsc,R_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dotsc,R_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
If <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota(D)=R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota(D)=R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[D-D_{0}]=[D_{i}-D_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[D-D_{0}]=[D_{i}-D_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[D-D_{i}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[D-D_{i}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\lvert D_{i}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\lvert D_{i}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Observe that if <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the hypotheses
of either Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> or <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
then they satisfy the hypotheses of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm18" title="Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>
and therefore <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into the union of finitely many complete linear series as in (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Proofs of Theorems <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
and their corollaries</h2>

<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the statement of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
By Proposition <a href="#Thmthm18" title="Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>,
<math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> has a finite decomposition, as in (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)
where <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dotsc,D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dotsc,D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are effective degree <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors.
If <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then, by Corollary <a href="#Thmthm16" title="Corollary 16. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>,
the complete linear series <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{i}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{i}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain primitive divisors.
On the other hand, if <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{i}\rvert=\{D_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{i}\rvert=\{D_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence there are only finitely many primitive degree <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose now that <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime.
Again, if <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then, by Corollary <a href="#Thmthm16" title="Corollary 16. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>,
the complete linear series <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{i}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{i}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> does not
contain irreducible divisors. The theorem follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be hyperelliptic of genus <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
This has gonality <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose either of
hypotheses (a), (b) of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>
is satisfied.
Then <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the hypotheses
of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
In particular, if <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is odd then <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has finitely
many degree <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
By Proposition <a href="#Thmthm18" title="Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>,
we have that (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) holds
where <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dotsc,D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dotsc,D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite
set of effective degree <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> divisors on <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point and let
<math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding irreducible
divisor. Then <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\lvert D_{i}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\lvert D_{i}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>
for some <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\neq D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\neq D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and so by Lemma <a href="#Thmthm15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>
the point <math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-isolated.
It follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
that <math id="S6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> contains a subfield
of index <math id="S6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for all but
finitely many degree <math id="S6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
we have that <math id="S6.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> contains
a subfield of index <math id="S6.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from Proposition <a href="#Thmthm18" title="Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> and
Corollary <a href="#Thmthm17" title="Corollary 17. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> by trivial
modifications to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm11" title="Corollary 11. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is a straightforward modification of preceding
arguments.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>A Remark on Effectivity</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
or Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Then <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(d)}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
can be decomposed into a finite union of complete linear
systems as in (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Suppose that
we are able to explicitly compute the representatives
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Then we have an effective
strategy for computing all primitive degree <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.
Indeed, if <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{i}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{i}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>
contains no primitive divisors by Corollary <a href="#Thmthm16" title="Corollary 16. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.
We are left to consider
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{i}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{i}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{i}\rvert=\{D_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{i}\rvert=\{D_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and we simply need to test
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to determine if it is the Galois orbit
of a primitive degree <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point. Moreover, if <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(d,m)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(d,m)=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime then we can compute all degree <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points by a slight modification
of the strategy: if <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math> then simply
test <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for irreducibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that the decomposition (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In Proposition 18. ‣ 5. Decomposition of 
        
          
            
            C
            
              
              
                (
                d
                )
              
            
          
        
       into complete linear series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)
can often be computed using symmetric power Chabauty
(e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>) provided <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+d\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+d\leq g</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank of the Mordell–Weil group <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>A Remark on the Assumptions of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted previously, assumption (b) of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is satisfied for <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math>
whenever the Jacobian <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is simple.
We remark that almost all hyperelliptic curves have simple Jacobians,
in a sense that we make precise shortly.
For this we will need the following theorem of Zarhin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 21</span></span><span id="Thmthm21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Zarhin)<span id="Thmthm21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="char(k)\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">char(k)\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:Y^{2}=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:Y^{2}=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in k[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in k[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is a separable
polynomial of degree <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose
<math id="Thmthm21.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="char(k)\neq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">char(k)\neq 3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, and
that <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has Galois group either <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End(J)\cong\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End(J)\cong\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in particular <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely simple.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We fix a genus <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2g+1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set of all polynomials of degree <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> can be naturally identified with
<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>: here <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}=(a_{0},a_{1},\dotsc,a_{n})\in\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}=(a_{0},a_{1},\dotsc,a_{n})\in\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponds to the polynomial</p>
<table id="S6.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{a}}(X)\;=\;a_{n}X^{n}+a_{n-1}X^{n-1}+\cdots+a_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{a}}(X)\;=\;a_{n}X^{n}+a_{n-1}X^{n-1}+\cdots+a_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Hilbert’s irreducibility theorem asserts the existence of a thin set <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})-S</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathbf{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathbf{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible
of degree <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and has Galois group <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Chapter 9]</cite> for a statement of Hilbert’s irreducibility theorem
as well as the definition of thin sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, for <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝔸</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{A}^{n+1}(\mathbb{Q})-S</annotation></semantics></math>,
the genus <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> hyperelliptic curve <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{2}=f_{\mathbf{a}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{2}=f_{\mathbf{a}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> has
a simple Jacobian by Zarhin’s theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We point out that there is no shortage of
hyperelliptic curves satisfying the finite
Mordell–Weil group condition of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This immediately
follows from the preceding remarks together with the following Theorem of Yu
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Theorem 3]</cite>, applied with <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 22</span></span><span id="Thmthm22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Yu)<span id="Thmthm22.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be a number field with at least one real embedding.
Let <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in K[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in K[X]</annotation></semantics></math> be a separable degree <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial such that
<math id="Thmthm22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 3\pmod{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 3\pmod{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(f)\cong S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(f)\cong S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\;Y^{2}=f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\;Y^{2}=f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperelliptic curve over <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with Jacobian <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for every <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, there are infinitely many quadratic twists <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm22.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{F}_{2}}(\Sel_{2}(J_{b}/K))=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sel</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{F}_{2}}(\Sel_{2}(J_{b}/K))=r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Proof of Theorem  <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> relies on the following proposition, which is in fact
a consequence of Hilbert’s Irreducibility Theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 23</span></span><span id="Thmthm23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer.
Let <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> morphism of curves defined over <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is not a branch value for <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>,
and that the fibre <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a single
Galois orbit of points. Let <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in f^{-1}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in f^{-1}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose the extension <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive.
Then there is a thin set <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> such that for <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})-S</annotation></semantics></math>,
the fibre <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a single Galois orbit of points and,
for any <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\pi^{-1}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\pi^{-1}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, the extension
<math id="Thmthm23.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive of degree <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Before proving Proposition <a href="#Thmthm23" title="Proposition 23. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>
we show that it implies Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve of genus <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in C(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive of degree <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq g+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding irreducible divisor.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Riemann–Roch,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D)\;\geq\;d-g+1\;\geq\;2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D)\;\geq\;d-g+1\;\geq\;2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">It follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmthm15" title="Lemma 15. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>
that there is a degree <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> morphism
<math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined over <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(\infty)=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(\infty)=D</annotation></semantics></math>.
We apply Proposition <a href="#Thmthm23" title="Proposition 23. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> with <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\infty\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\infty\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The theorem follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm23" title="Proposition 23. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> makes use
of the relationship between primitive extensions and
primitive Galois groups. Whilst this relationship
is known, we are unable to find a convenient
reference, and we therefore give the details.
Let <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-empty set, and let <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be
a group acting transitively on <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trivial</span> partitions of <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
are <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{x\}:x\in X\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{x\}:x\in X\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A partition <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="S7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-stable</span>
if <math id="S7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(Z)\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(Z)\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S7.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe, as the action of <math id="S7.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive,
that <math id="S7.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> also acts transitively on any <math id="S7.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-stable partition <math id="S7.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and that any two elements of <math id="S7.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore have the same cardinality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acts imprimitively</span>
on <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> admits a <math id="S7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-stable non-trivial partition.
If <math id="S7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> does not have a <math id="S7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-stable non-trivial partition
then we say that <math id="S7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S7.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">acts primitively on <math id="S7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
The following lemma is an immediate consequence of this
definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 24</span></span><span id="Thmthm24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group acting transitively on a set <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a subgroup of <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
acts primitively on <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts primitively on <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is well-known, and in particular
implies that <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> act primitively on <math id="S7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dotsc,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dotsc,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for <math id="S7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25</span></span><span id="Thmthm25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group acting <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-transitively on a set <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the action is primitive.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-stable partition of <math id="S7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose <math id="S7.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> has at least two
elements. We would like to show that <math id="S7.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct, and let <math id="S7.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in X</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct from <math id="S7.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is some <math id="S7.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in G</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S7.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(a)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(a)=a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(b)=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(b)=c</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S7.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in Y\cap\sigma(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in Y\cap\sigma(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> which forces <math id="S7.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(Y)=Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(Y)=Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore <math id="S7.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S7.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 26</span></span><span id="Thmthm26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group acting transitively on a set <math id="Thmthm26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
The action is imprimitive if and only if there is
a proper subset <math id="Thmthm26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
with at least two elements, such that</span></p>
<table id="S7.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\sigma\in G,\qquad\text{if $\sigma(Y)\cap Y\neq\emptyset$ then $\sigma(Y)=Y$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> then </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\sigma\in G,\qquad\text{if $\sigma(Y)\cap Y\neq\emptyset$ then $\sigma(Y)=Y$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a <math id="S7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-stable non-trivial partition <math id="S7.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> we can take
<math id="S7.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to be any element of <math id="S7.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S7.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is a partition, <math id="S7.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> clearly satisfies
(<a href="#S7.E12" title="In Lemma 26. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), and as <math id="S7.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-trivial, <math id="S7.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper subset of <math id="S7.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
with at least two elements.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose <math id="S7.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper subset of <math id="S7.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> containing
at least two elements and satisfying (<a href="#S7.E12" title="In Lemma 26. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
We easily check that <math id="S7.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{\tau(Y):\tau\in G\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{\tau(Y):\tau\in G\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S7.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-stable
non-trivial partition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 27</span></span><span id="Thmthm27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite group acting transitively on a non-empty finite set <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, and write <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Stab(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Stab(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for the stabilizer
of <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The action of <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is imprimitive
if and only if <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Stab(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Stab(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-maximal
proper subgroup of <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> and assume the existence of a subgroup <math id="S7.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Stab(x)\subsetneq H\subsetneq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊊</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>⊊</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Stab(x)\subsetneq H\subsetneq G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\{\tau(x):\tau\in H\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\{\tau(x):\tau\in H\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S7.p11.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\#Y=[H:\Stab(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>#</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>Stab</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#Y=[H:\Stab(x)]</annotation></semantics></math> and so
<math id="S7.p11.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\leq\#Y&lt;[G:\Stab(x)]=\#X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>#</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>Stab</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>#</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq\#Y&lt;[G:\Stab(x)]=\#X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, suppose <math id="S7.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(Y)\cap Y\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(Y)\cap Y\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\sigma(Y)\cap Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\sigma(Y)\cap Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there are <math id="S7.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}\in H</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\tau_{2}(x)=z=\tau_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\tau_{2}(x)=z=\tau_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S7.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}^{-1}\sigma\tau_{2}\in\Stab(x)\subseteq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}^{-1}\sigma\tau_{2}\in\Stab(x)\subseteq H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S7.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in H</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S7.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(Y)=Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(Y)=Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore (<a href="#S7.E12" title="In Lemma 26. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is satisfied and so the action is imprimitive.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose the existence of a proper subset <math id="S7.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with at least two elements satisfying (<a href="#S7.E12" title="In Lemma 26. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
As the action is transitive, we may in fact suppose that <math id="S7.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let
<math id="S7.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\{\sigma\in G\;:\&gt;\sigma(Y)=Y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\{\sigma\in G\;:\&gt;\sigma(Y)=Y\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S7.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive, <math id="S7.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper subgroup of <math id="S7.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S7.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Stab(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Stab(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in <math id="S7.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}\neq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}\neq x</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is some
<math id="S7.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)=x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)=x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S7.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(Y)=Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(Y)=Y</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so <math id="S7.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in H</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S7.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\notin\Stab(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\notin\Stab(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows
that <math id="S7.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Stab(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Stab(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper in <math id="S7.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and so is non-maximal
as a subgroup of <math id="S7.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 28</span></span><span id="Thmthm28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> be a number field and let <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be its Galois
closure over <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d=[K:\mathbb{Q}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=[K:\mathbb{Q}]</annotation></semantics></math>
and let <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}\in\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}\in\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Galois
conjugates of <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acts primitively
on <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
the extension <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
acts transitively on <math id="S7.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. We let <math id="S7.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\theta\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\theta\in X</annotation></semantics></math>
and note that the stabilizer <math id="S7.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Stab(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Stab</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Stab(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact
<math id="S7.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\tilde{K}/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\tilde{K}/K)</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Galois correspondence, <math id="S7.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is imprimitive
if and only if the subgroup <math id="S7.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\tilde{K}/K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\tilde{K}/K)</annotation></semantics></math> is proper
and non-maximal in <math id="S7.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which by Lemma <a href="#Thmthm27" title="Lemma 27. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>
if equivalent to the action of <math id="S7.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> being imprimitive.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The following result was assumed in the introduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 29</span></span><span id="Thmthm29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve, let <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
be a degree <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with Galois group <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">The lemma follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmthm28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>,
and for this we need the fact that <math id="S7.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S7.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> act primitively on <math id="S7.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dotsc,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dotsc,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is trivially true if <math id="S7.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S7.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>
follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmthm25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmthm23" title="Proposition 23. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">By composing <math id="S7.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with a suitable automorphism of <math id="S7.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
we may suppose that <math id="S7.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="S7.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}=\mathbb{Q}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}=\mathbb{Q}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> for the function field of <math id="S7.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
We may regard <math id="S7.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as a non-constant element of <math id="S7.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and with this interpretation <math id="S7.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(f)=\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(f)=\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is a subfield of <math id="S7.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
of index <math id="S7.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">By hypothesis, <math id="S7.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> is not a branch value for <math id="S7.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S7.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a single Galois orbit containing <math id="S7.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="S7.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=f^{*}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=f^{*}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> which we think of as a degree <math id="S7.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> place of <math id="S7.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>,
unramified in the extension <math id="S7.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{D}=\{h\in\mathbb{K}\;:\;\ord_{D}(h)\geq 0\},\qquad\mathfrak{m}_{D}=\{h\in\mathbb{K}\;:\;\ord_{D}(h)\geq 1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ord</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ord</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{D}=\{h\in\mathbb{K}\;:\;\ord_{D}(h)\geq 0\},\qquad\mathfrak{m}_{D}=\{h\in\mathbb{K}\;:\;\ord_{D}(h)\geq 1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">for the valuation ring of <math id="S7.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and its maximal ideal. Then the residue field
<math id="S7.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{D}/\mathfrak{m}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{D}/\mathfrak{m}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with <math id="S7.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> where
the identification is given by <math id="S7.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+\mathfrak{m}_{D}\mapsto g(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝔪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+\mathfrak{m}_{D}\mapsto g(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now fix <math id="S7.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in K</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is some <math id="S7.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{O}_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{O}_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S7.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(P)=\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(P)=\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S7.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S7.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S7.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>,
there is a polynomial <math id="S7.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,V)\in\mathbb{Q}[U,V]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,V)\in\mathbb{Q}[U,V]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,V)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}(V)U^{i},\qquad a_{i}(V)\in\mathbb{Q}[V]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,V)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}(V)U^{i},\qquad a_{i}(V)\in\mathbb{Q}[V]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p17.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text">of degree <math id="S7.p17.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\mid d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mid d</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S7.p17.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(a_{0}(V),\dotsc,a_{n}(V))=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(a_{0}(V),\dotsc,a_{n}(V))=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S7.p17.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(g,f)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(g,f)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, <math id="S7.p17.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta,0)=F(g(P),f(P))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta,0)=F(g(P),f(P))=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and so <math id="S7.p17.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is a root of the polynomial <math id="S7.p17.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,0)\in\mathbb{Q}[U]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,0)\in\mathbb{Q}[U]</annotation></semantics></math>;
this polynomial is non-zero as <math id="S7.p17.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gcd(a_{0}(V),\dotsc,a_{n}(V))=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gcd(a_{0}(V),\dotsc,a_{n}(V))=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S7.p17.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S7.p17.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S7.p17.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=d</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S7.p17.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,V)</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible over <math id="S7.p17.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular <math id="S7.p17.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,V)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,V)=0</annotation></semantics></math> is a (possibly singular) plane model for <math id="S7.p17.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S7.p17.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
is absolutely irreducible, <math id="S7.p17.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,V)</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible over <math id="S7.p17.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="S7.p17.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Galois closure of <math id="S7.p17.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S7.p17.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the roots of <math id="S7.p17.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,f)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,f)=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p17.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
then <math id="S7.p17.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}=\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})(g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}=\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})(g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the algebraic curve associated to <math id="S7.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be an extension of the place <math id="S7.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S7.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is unramified in <math id="S7.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the place <math id="S7.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is unramified
in the Galois closure <math id="S7.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> (see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, Corollary III.8.4]</cite>).
We claim that <math id="S7.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\in\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\in\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, note that
<math id="S7.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\Gal(\overline{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\Gal(\overline{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1}))</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the <math id="S7.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus we would like to show <math id="S7.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ord_{\tilde{D}}(\tau(g))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ord</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ord_{\tilde{D}}(\tau(g))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S7.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in G</annotation></semantics></math>. However,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ord_{\tilde{D}}(\tau(g))\;=\;\ord_{\tau^{-1}(\tilde{D})}(g)\;=\;\ord_{D}(g)\;\geq\;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ord</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ord</mi><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ord</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ord_{\tilde{D}}(\tau(g))\;=\;\ord_{\tau^{-1}(\tilde{D})}(g)\;=\;\ord_{D}(g)\;\geq\;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the second equality follows as <math id="S7.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{-1}(\tilde{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{-1}(\tilde{D})</annotation></semantics></math> is an unramified place of <math id="S7.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> above <math id="S7.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
This proves the claim.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">The place <math id="S7.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponds to a Galois orbit of points on <math id="S7.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
and we let <math id="S7.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be a representative point chosen above <math id="S7.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write <math id="S7.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the decomposition group corresponding to <math id="S7.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S7.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\Gal(\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\Gal(\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1}))</annotation></semantics></math>; by definition this is</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}=\{\sigma\in G\;:\;\sigma(\mathfrak{m}_{\tilde{D}})=\mathfrak{m}_{\tilde{D}}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}=\{\sigma\in G\;:\;\sigma(\mathfrak{m}_{\tilde{D}})=\mathfrak{m}_{\tilde{D}}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">As <math id="S7.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is unramified in
the Galois closure <math id="S7.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the theory of decomposition groups
allows us to identify the
decomposition group <math id="S7.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the Galois closure of <math id="S7.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we shall in fact
need the details of the precise identification. Let <math id="S7.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We associate <math id="S7.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}\in\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}\in\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
We let <math id="S7.p19.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\tilde{K}\cong\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{D}}/\mathfrak{m}_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\tilde{K}\cong\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{D}}/\mathfrak{m}_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S7.p19.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=h(\tilde{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=h(\tilde{P})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S7.p19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{O}_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and we define <math id="S7.p19.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(\gamma)=\sigma(h)(\tilde{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(\gamma)=\sigma(h)(\tilde{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. It immediately
follows from the definition of <math id="S7.p19.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="S7.p19.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined. The map <math id="S7.p19.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}\rightarrow\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}\rightarrow\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
given by <math id="S7.p19.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\mapsto\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\mapsto\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact an isomorphism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, Theorem III.8.2]</cite>,
and from now on we shall
identify <math id="S7.p19.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p19.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> via this identification.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the conjugates of <math id="S7.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we take <math id="S7.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}=\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}=\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now <math id="S7.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=g(P)=g(\tilde{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=g(P)=g(\tilde{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is some <math id="S7.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\in G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\in G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S7.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(\theta)=\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(\theta)=\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore <math id="S7.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}(g)(\tilde{P})=\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}(g)(\tilde{P})=\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus there is a conjugate
of <math id="S7.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="S7.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As the <math id="S7.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise distinct, we may after reordering the <math id="S7.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> suppose that
<math id="S7.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}(\tilde{P})=\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}(\tilde{P})=\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <math id="S7.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})=G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gal(\tilde{K}/\mathbb{Q})=G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of <math id="S7.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\Gal(\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕂</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\Gal(\tilde{\mathbb{K}}/\mathbb{Q}(\mathbb{P}^{1}))</annotation></semantics></math>
and both act on <math id="S7.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S7.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive, Lemma <a href="#Thmthm28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> tells us that
<math id="S7.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts primitively on the
<math id="S7.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore on the <math id="S7.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus by Lemma <a href="#Thmthm24" title="Lemma 24. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, the action
of <math id="S7.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally we apply Hilbert’s Irreducibility Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Chapter 9]</cite> to <math id="S7.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S7.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> we shall make a standard abuse of language
and speak of the decomposition group <math id="S7.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> by which we mean the
decomposition group <math id="S7.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\tilde{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\tilde{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> for any point <math id="S7.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}\in\tilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}\in\tilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> above <math id="S7.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
As usual, <math id="S7.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is only defined up to conjugation in <math id="S7.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, Hilbert’s Irreducibility Theorem applied to <math id="S7.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:C\rightarrow\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
asserts the existence of a thin set <math id="S7.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which includes all branch values, such that
<math id="S7.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\beta}=G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\beta}=G</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})-S</annotation></semantics></math>. We enlarge <math id="S7.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by adjoining
finitely many values in <math id="S7.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>: the roots of <math id="S7.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{d}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{d}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>
(which is the leading coefficient of <math id="S7.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> regarded as an element
of <math id="S7.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[V][U]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[V][U]</annotation></semantics></math>); the <math id="S7.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate of any singular point
of the plane model <math id="S7.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,V)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,V)=0</annotation></semantics></math>; and the point <math id="S7.p21.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
As we have added finitely many points to the set <math id="S7.p21.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> it remains thin.
Let <math id="S7.p21.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{P}^{1}(\mathbb{Q})-S</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p21.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\dotsc,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\dotsc,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the roots of <math id="S7.p21.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(U,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(U,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p21.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(\phi_{1},\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(\phi_{1},\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>; this is a smooth point on
the plane model, and so may be regarded as a point on <math id="S7.p21.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p21.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
be a point of <math id="S7.p21.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> above <math id="S7.p21.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then as before, we can identify the
action of <math id="S7.p21.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p21.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{1},\dotsc,\phi_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{1},\dotsc,\phi_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the action of <math id="S7.p21.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
on <math id="S7.p21.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S7.p21.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is acting transitively and primitively on
<math id="S7.p21.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{1},\dotsc,g_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have that <math id="S7.p21.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\beta}=\Gal(\mathbb{Q}(f^{-1}(\beta))/\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\beta}=\Gal(\mathbb{Q}(f^{-1}(\beta))/\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is acting
transitively and primitively on <math id="S7.p21.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{1},\dotsc,\phi_{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{1},\dotsc,\phi_{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the Galois action
on the fibre <math id="S7.p21.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(\beta)=\{(\phi_{1},\beta),\dotsc,(\phi_{d},\beta)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(\beta)=\{(\phi_{1},\beta),\dotsc,(\phi_{d},\beta)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is
primitive and, by Lemma <a href="#Thmthm28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 7. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>, the field <math id="S7.p21.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(Q)=\mathbb{Q}(\phi_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(Q)=\mathbb{Q}(\phi_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive of
degree <math id="S7.p21.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
The proposition follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve and let <math id="S7.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq g+1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S7.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of <math id="S7.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> asserts the existence
of infinitely many primitive degree <math id="S7.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S7.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
provided there is at least one. However, the existence
of a primitive degree <math id="S7.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point is not guaranteed,
as illustrated by the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 30</span></span><span id="Thmthm30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> be even. Let <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+1</annotation></semantics></math>
genus <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curve defined over <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\;:\;Y^{2}\;=\;a_{2g+1}X^{2g+1}+a_{2g}X^{2g}+\cdots+a_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\;:\;Y^{2}\;=\;a_{2g+1}X^{2g+1}+a_{2g}X^{2g}+\cdots+a_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm30.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm30.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})=0</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian of <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has no points of degree <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+1</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S7.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for the single point at infinity
on the given model. Write <math id="S7.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}=(g+1)\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}=(g+1)\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S7.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a double pole at <math id="S7.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S7.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,X,\dots,X^{g/2}\in L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,X,\dots,X^{g/2}\in L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that <math id="S7.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,X,\dotsc,X^{g/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,X,\dotsc,X^{g/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis for <math id="S7.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first explain how our claim implies the lemma.
Let <math id="S7.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be an effective degree <math id="S7.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+1</annotation></semantics></math> divisor.
Since <math id="S7.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S7.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D-D_{0}=\divv(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D-D_{0}=\divv(f)</annotation></semantics></math>
for some <math id="S7.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,
<math id="S7.p23.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}X+\cdots+\alpha_{g/2}X^{g/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}X+\cdots+\alpha_{g/2}X^{g/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S7.p23.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0},\dotsc,\alpha_{g/2}\in\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0},\dotsc,\alpha_{g/2}\in\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular <math id="S7.p23.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L(g\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L(g\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D-\infty\;=\;D_{0}+\divv(f)-\infty\;=\;g\infty+\divv(f)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D-\infty\;=\;D_{0}+\divv(f)-\infty\;=\;g\infty+\divv(f)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p23.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p23.2.1" class="ltx_text">is effective. Hence <math id="S7.p23.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is reducible. It follows that <math id="S7.p23.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has no degree <math id="S7.p23.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+1</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove our claim.
Since <math id="S7.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,X,\dots,X^{g/2}\in L(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,X,\dots,X^{g/2}\in L(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, our claim is equivalent to showing that
<math id="S7.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})\leq g/2+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})\leq g/2+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S7.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the Riemann–Roch theorem
immediately implies that <math id="S7.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})=2=g/2+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})=2=g/2+1</annotation></semantics></math> as required.
We may therefore suppose <math id="S7.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, and as <math id="S7.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is even, <math id="S7.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows from the Riemann–Roch
theorem (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) that <math id="S7.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(K_{C}-D_{0})=\ell(D_{0})-2\geq g/2-1&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(K_{C}-D_{0})=\ell(D_{0})-2\geq g/2-1&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S7.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a special divisor.
By Clifford’s theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmthm13" title="Theorem 13 (Clifford). ‣ 2. Proof of Theorem ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>)
we have <math id="S7.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})\leq g/2+3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})\leq g/2+3/2</annotation></semantics></math>. However, since <math id="S7.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S7.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
is an integer, we have <math id="S7.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{0})\leq g/2+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{0})\leq g/2+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p25.1" class="ltx_p">In a positive direction, we can use Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>
to construct curves with infinitely many primitive points.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 31</span></span><span id="Thmthm31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=g+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a hyperelliptic curve <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
of genus <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with infinitely many primitive degree <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S7.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> be any primitive number field of degree <math id="S7.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1},\dotsc,\theta_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the conjugates of <math id="S7.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>
in a fixed Galois closure <math id="S7.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choose a rational number <math id="S7.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S7.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\alpha\neq\theta_{i}+\theta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\alpha\neq\theta_{i}+\theta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for any pair <math id="S7.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i,~j\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i,~j\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\theta-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\theta-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The conjugates of <math id="S7.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>
are <math id="S7.p26.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}=\theta_{i}-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}=\theta_{i}-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p26.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>,
and satisfy <math id="S7.p26.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}\neq\pm\phi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}\neq\pm\phi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for any pair <math id="S7.p26.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p26.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S7.p26.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})\subseteq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})\subseteq K</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S7.p26.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is primitive, either <math id="S7.p26.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S7.p26.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})=K</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if <math id="S7.p26.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S7.p26.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)=\mathbb{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}(\theta)=\mathbb{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> has degree at most
<math id="S7.p26.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the fact that <math id="S7.p26.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> has
degree <math id="S7.p26.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=g+1\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=g+1\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S7.p26.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\mathbb{Q}(\phi^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p26.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathbb{Q}[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathbb{Q}[X]</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimal polynomial
of <math id="S7.p26.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which must be
irreducible of degree <math id="S7.p26.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S7.p26.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=f(X^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=f(X^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The roots of <math id="S7.p26.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S7.p26.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\phi_{1},\dotsc,\pm\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\phi_{1},\dotsc,\pm\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
which are pairwise distinct and hence <math id="S7.p26.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is separable
of degree <math id="S7.p26.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d=2g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d=2g+2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p26.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be the
genus <math id="S7.p26.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> hyperelliptic
curve</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\;:\;Y^{2}\;=\;h(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\;:\;Y^{2}\;=\;h(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note that this has the primitive
degree <math id="S7.p26.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> point <math id="S7.p26.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence by
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm12" title="Theorem 12. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> there are
infinitely many primitive degree <math id="S7.p26.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Low degree primitive points on some <math id="S8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mazur <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> showed that all rational points on <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>
are cuspidal for prime <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.
Merel’s uniform boundedness theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> asserts that for prime <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>,
and for <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3^{d/2}+1)^{2}\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3^{d/2}+1)^{2}\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>, the only
degree <math id="S8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> are cuspidal.
We now know, for each <math id="S8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq d\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq d\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, the exact set
of primes <math id="S8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> non-cuspidal points,
thanks to the efforts of Kamienny <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, Parent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>,
Derickx, Kamienny, Stein and Stoll <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and Khawaja <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
Less is known about the low degree points on <math id="S8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for composite <math id="S8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>,
though several authors consider the somewhat easier problem of determining
the values of <math id="S8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> such <math id="S8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> has infinitely many
degree <math id="S8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points for given small <math id="S8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>
for two different approaches to studying this problem).
Example <a href="#Thmthm10" title="Example 10. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> illustrates how our results can be applied
to modular curves <math id="S8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> provided the analytic rank of <math id="S8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="S8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and we have information about the quotients or gonality of <math id="S8.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LMFDB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> contains a database of modular curves <math id="S8.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S8.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq N\leq 70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq N\leq 70</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S8.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61</annotation></semantics></math> of
these curves the Jacobian <math id="S8.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=J_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=J_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> has analytic rank <math id="S8.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows from a theorem of Kato <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Corollary 14.3]</cite>
that the Mordell–Weil group <math id="S8.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.
We are able to apply Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> to around half of these curves
in order to deduce the finiteness of primitive points of certain low degrees.
We note that it is common for <math id="S8.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> to cover multiple curves, and in these instances we apply
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> to the covered curve <math id="S8.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that
gives the most generous range for <math id="S8.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in inequality (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In Theorem 8. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
We record the results in Table <a href="#S8.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 8. Low degree primitive points on some 
        
          
            ⁢
            
              
              X
              1
            
            
              
              
                (
                N
                ) ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
