<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>The table summarizes our conclusions upon applying
Theorem <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> to <math id="S8.T1.m91" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for the values of <math id="S8.T1.m92" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> in the first column.
Here <math id="S8.T1.m93" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the genus of <math id="S8.T1.m94" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>; the integer <math id="S8.T1.m95" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the degree of the morphism
<math id="S8.T1.m96" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}(N)\rightarrow C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}(N)\rightarrow C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S8.T1.m97" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the genus of <math id="S8.T1.m98" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sixth column gives the values of <math id="S8.T1.m99" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> furnished by the theorem
for which there are only finitely many
points of degree <math id="S8.T1.m100" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. The final column gives the values of <math id="S8.T1.m101" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
(not appearing in the previous column) for which the theorem
asserts that there are only finitely primitive degree <math id="S8.T1.m102" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem 3.1]</cite> the authors give
a complete list
of <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> has analytic rank <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are in total <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>83</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83</annotation></semantics></math> such values of <math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>,
the largest of which is <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=180" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=180</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Low degree primitive points on some <math id="S9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The computational study of quadratic points on modular curves is an active area of research
(see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, to name but
a few works). Comparatively less is known about points defined over number
fields of higher degree.
Still, there is reason to be hopeful.
Establishing the modularity of all elliptic curves over totally real cubic fields <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and totally real quartic fields not containing <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{5}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> required the study of cubic, and quartic points on certain modular curves.
Banwait and Derickx
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> have determined all cubic points on <math id="S9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{41,47,59,71\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>41</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>47</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>59</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>71</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{41,47,59,71\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Box, Gajović, and Goodman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> have determined all cubic points on <math id="S9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{53,57,61,65,67,73\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>53</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>57</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>61</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>65</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>67</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>73</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{53,57,61,65,67,73\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and all quartic points on <math id="S9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(65)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>65</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(65)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A famous theorem of Ogg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> asserts that there are <math id="S9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="S9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for which which <math id="S9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> is hyperelliptic.
Of these, the only one for which <math id="S9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(N)(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(N)(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite is <math id="S9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=37</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining <math id="S9.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> values are</p>
<ul id="S9.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">genus <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=22</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>31</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">genus <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=30</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>33</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">genus <math id="S9.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S9.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=47</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">genus <math id="S9.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S9.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=46</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">genus <math id="S9.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S9.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>71</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=71</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For these <math id="S9.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, the quadratic points on <math id="S9.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> have been
determined by Bruin and Najman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
It is easy to apply Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to these curves
and derive conclusions about algebraic points of degree <math id="S9.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of <math id="S9.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, consider <math id="S9.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math> with genus <math id="S9.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Corollary <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we know that there are only finitely many points on <math id="S9.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math> of degrees <math id="S9.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>,
and finitely many primitive points of degrees <math id="S9.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. We point out that we can in fact go further
and compute these finite sets of points, as sketched in Remark <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1. A Remark on Effectivity ‣ 6. Proofs of Theorems  and 
and their corollaries ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>.
We illustrate this by giving some details of the computation of
primitive degree <math id="S9.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S9.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math>, making use
of information found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> concerning
the model and the Mordell–Weil group. A model for <math id="S9.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S9.p1.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)\;:\;Y^{2}\;=\;X^{14}+4X^{13}-2X^{12}-38X^{11}-77X^{10}-26X^{9}+111X^{8}\\
+148X^{7}+X^{6}-122X^{5}-70X^{4}+30X^{3}+40X^{2}+4X-11." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo rspace="0.838em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.838em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.838em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>14</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>13</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>12</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>38</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>77</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>26</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>111</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>148</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>122</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)\;:\;Y^{2}\;=\;X^{14}+4X^{13}-2X^{12}-38X^{11}-77X^{10}-26X^{9}+111X^{8}\\
+148X^{7}+X^{6}-122X^{5}-70X^{4}+30X^{3}+40X^{2}+4X-11.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The only rational points are the two rational points at infinity which we denote by
<math id="S9.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> (these are in fact the two cusps of <math id="S9.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math>). Write</p>
<table id="S9.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}=\infty_{+}-\infty_{-},\qquad D_{\infty}=\infty_{+}+\infty_{-}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}=\infty_{+}-\infty_{-},\qquad D_{\infty}=\infty_{+}+\infty_{-}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Then,</p>
<table id="S9.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})\;=\;(\mathbb{Z}/35\mathbb{Z})\cdot[D_{0}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>35</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})\;=\;(\mathbb{Z}/35\mathbb{Z})\cdot[D_{0}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S9.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=J_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=J_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a primitive degree <math id="S9.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> point on <math id="S9.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S9.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
be the corresponding effective irreducible degree <math id="S9.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> divisor. Hence
<math id="S9.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[D-3D_{\infty}]\in J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[D-3D_{\infty}]\in J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that</p>
<table id="S9.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\lvert D_{a}\rvert,\qquad D_{a}=a\cdot D_{0}+3D_{\infty},\qquad-17\leq a\leq 17." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.778em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\lvert D_{a}\rvert,\qquad D_{a}=a\cdot D_{0}+3D_{\infty},\qquad-17\leq a\leq 17.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p1.7" class="ltx_p">We find that <math id="S9.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{a})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S9.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>, is <math id="S9.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>, is <math id="S9.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pm 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pm 2</annotation></semantics></math> and is <math id="S9.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>
for all other values of <math id="S9.p1.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S9.p1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{a})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{a})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> then, by Corollary <a href="#Thmthm16" title="Corollary 16. ‣ 4. Primitive Divisors in Complete Linear Series ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>,
we know that <math id="S9.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert D_{a}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert D_{a}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>
does not contain primitive divisors.
Thus <math id="S9.p1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\lvert D_{a}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\lvert D_{a}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-17\leq a\leq-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-17\leq a\leq-3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S9.p1.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq a\leq 17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq a\leq 17</annotation></semantics></math> whence <math id="S9.p1.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(D_{a})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(D_{a})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each of these values, <math id="S9.p1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(D_{a})=\mathbb{Q}\cdot f_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(D_{a})=\mathbb{Q}\cdot f_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.p1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-zero function on
<math id="S9.p1.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="S9.p1.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\lvert D_{a}\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\lvert D_{a}\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S9.p1.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=D_{a}+\divv(f_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=D_{a}+\divv(f_{a})</annotation></semantics></math>. We obtain <math id="S9.p1.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> potential possibilities for the divisor <math id="S9.p1.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
We find that for <math id="S9.p1.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pm 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pm 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the divisor <math id="S9.p1.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{a}+\divv(f_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{a}+\divv(f_{a})</annotation></semantics></math> is reducible, and for <math id="S9.p1.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\pm 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\pm 5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p1.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p1.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 12</annotation></semantics></math>,
the divisor <math id="S9.p1.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{a}+\divv(f_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>div</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{a}+\divv(f_{a})</annotation></semantics></math> is the Galois orbit of an imprimitive point. The remaining <math id="S9.p1.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> values
of <math id="S9.p1.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> yield the Galois orbit of a primitive degree <math id="S9.p1.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> point. We conclude that there
are precisely <math id="S9.p1.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> primitive degree <math id="S9.p1.m71" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> points on <math id="S9.p1.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(71)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>71</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(71)</annotation></semantics></math> up to Galois conjugacy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We carried out similar computations for the hyperelliptic <math id="S9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{46,47,59,71\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>46</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>47</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>59</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>71</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{46,47,59,71\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for degrees <math id="S9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
in the range <math id="S9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq d\leq\min(g,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq d\leq\min(g,6)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of <math id="S9.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The outcome of these computations is summarized in Table <a href="#S9.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 9. Low degree primitive points on some 
        
          
            ⁢
            
              
              X
              0
            
            
              
              
                (
                N
                ) ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Here
we were helped by the fact
that these values of <math id="S9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, the Mordell–Weil group <math id="S9.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(N)(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(N)(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> has been computed by Bruin and Najman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Furthermore, models for the curves are readily available in <span id="S9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Magma</span>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>
via the <span id="S9.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Small Modular Curve package</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In view of Corollary <a href="#Thmthm11" title="Corollary 11. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Primitive Algebraic Points on Curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, it is natural to also consider bielliptic <math id="S9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Bars <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> shows that <math id="S9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> is bielliptic for precisely <math id="S9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math> values of <math id="S9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Of these,
<math id="S9.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> has analytic rank <math id="S9.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> of these values:</p>
<ul id="S9.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S9.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">genus <math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=22</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
