<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>This table gives the conclusions of our
computations of primitive points on <math id="S9.T2.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>
of certain low degrees <math id="S9.T2.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and for the values of <math id="S9.T2.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the
first column. Here <math id="S9.T2.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus
of <math id="S9.T2.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.T2.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact the structure
of the Mordell–Weil group where <math id="S9.T2.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=J_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=J_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The table gives the number of primitive degree <math id="S9.T2.m71" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points
on <math id="S9.T2.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> up to Galois conjugacy. The symbol <math id="S9.T2.m73" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that
our method is inapplicable for that particular <math id="S9.T2.m74" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.T2.m75" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
